{#
  What is wrong *together*.

  The findings list ranks problems one at a time; this section is the only
  place a document says that three of them form a route. Ordered by hops rather
  than by severity, because a shorter route is both likelier to be walked and
  cheaper to sever — and the line that matters to whoever acts on this report
  is the last one in each block, which names the single link to cut.
#}
<div class="section">
<h2>Attack paths</h2>

{% if not report.attack_paths %}
<p class="muted">
  No route was found from an internet-facing asset to a sensitive one. What
  counts as sensitive is declared per environment in CloudGuard, so an estate
  where nothing has been classified will also report none — this is not by
  itself an all-clear.
</p>
{% else %}
<p class="small muted">
  Each route runs from something reachable to something worth taking. Fewer
  hops is worse: the route is shorter to walk and there are fewer places to
  break it.
</p>

{% for path in report.attack_paths %}
<div class="finding">
  <div class="finding-head">
    <span class="finding-title">{{ path.entry }} → {{ path.target }}</span>
    <span class="muted small">{{ path.hops }} hop{{ '' if path.hops == 1 else 's' }}</span>
  </div>
  <ol class="small">
    {% for step in path.steps %}
    <li>{{ step }}</li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>
  {% if path.cheapest_break %}
  <p class="small"><strong>Cut here:</strong> {{ path.cheapest_break }}</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
</div>
